 3. 🗽 Scoop: New &#x27;28 kingmaker 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 NYC Mayor Zohran Mamdani speaks at an election night event in Manhattan last week. Photo: Angelina Katsanis/Bloomberg via Getty Images Holly Otterbein popped this scoop last night in Axios 2028 , our weekly newsletter on the forces shaping the presidential campaign: 

 New York Mayor Zohran Mamdani can't be president, so he's trying to be the next best thing: a progressive kingmaker for 2028.

 Mamdani has been meeting privately with union leaders, urgently talking up the need for a labor-aligned or leftist candidate to run for the White House, four people familiar with the talks told Axios. Why it matters: Behind closed doors, Mamdani has made a striking admission that helps explain why he's so eager to huddle with allies about 2028: He's uncertain whether New York Rep. Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez, his close political partner, will launch a White House run, the sources said.

 🔎 Zoom in: Mamdani — who's ineligible to be president because he was born in Uganda — wants to coordinate with progressive leaders partly to prepare to unite against any leading centrist presidential contender they aren't happy with, according to one of the sources who has spoken with the mayor.

 Mamdani "wants the progressive ecosystem to mobilize quickly, strategically and efficiently if a moderate candidate is clearly on the rise," the person said, pointing to how former President Barack Obama appeared to help Joe Biden consolidate support against Bernie Sanders in 2020. Share this story ... Get Axios 2028 . 

 
 
