 4. ⏱️ Trump: Deportations &quot;too tough&quot; 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 "60 Minutes" correspondent Ross Douthat interviews President Trump on Friday at the White House. Photo: CBS News In his first sit-down interview since his midterm wipeout, President Trump told "60 Minutes" correspondent Ross Douthat that the administration's deportation policy "might be too tough" and left the door open to a deal with Democrats on immigration.

 An hour after the interview, Trump replaced White House chief of staff Susie Wiles with Stephen Miller, the architect of his immigration crackdown. Trump acknowledged that his crackdown had swept up people who had lived and worked in the U.S. for years, including hotel and landscaping workers whose only violation was entering the country illegally.

 Trump said the idea would be to "get the killers out. Get all ... the rough ones out. Get the drug dealers out. But let the people that are here, and in some cases here for 10 and 15 years, work a deal for them." 💬 More Trump quotes: 

 Iran war: Trump said he'd go to war with Iran again if given the choice: "My answer is an emphatic yes because you can't let Iran have a nuclear weapon. I think, historically, this will be a very good decision. Is it a good decision for today and tomorrow? Maybe not. But you know, I have to go with what's right for our country." AI regulations: Trump said he'd be willing to work with Democrats on safeguards for AI. "I don't mind guardrails," he said. "But I don't wanna put us in a deep second position. We're leading number one. And China has no guardrails." Going to heaven: Asked whether he could forgive political enemies such as Nancy Pelosi and James Comey if it would mean getting to enter heaven, Trump said: "I probably cannot do that, no, because I do hate some of my enemies and I always will." Watch the interview ... Extended version ... Full transcript . 

 
 
